#doc (Manual) "The Orlik–Solomon theorem from the axioms of cohomology" =>
%%%
shortTitle := "Orlik–Solomon from axioms"
authors := ["Adam Topaz", "Claude", "Codex"]
%%%

This book explains a formalization, in Lean and Mathlib, of the Orlik–Solomon structure theorem for the cohomology of complements of hyperplane arrangements, in affine space and in projective space.
Both theorems are proved, for every cohomology theory that satisfies the axioms.
The book is written for a working mathematician who wants to know what exactly is proved, from what, and how.
No knowledge of Lean is needed: the prose stands on its own, and the Lean statements are shown so that you can compare them with the mathematics.

{statusSummary}

*The setting.*
Fix a field $`k` and a finite set $`\mathcal{A}` of distinct hyperplanes defined over $`k` in an affine space of dimension $`n`, and let $`U_{\mathcal{A}}` be the complement of their union.
The classical theorem of Orlik and Solomon presents the cohomology of the complement of a complex arrangement by generators and relations that can be read off from the incidences of the hyperplanes.
Here the cohomology theory is not fixed.
It is any theory with bigraded groups $`H^{p,w}`, where $`p` is the cohomological degree and $`w` is the Tate twist, that satisfies the axioms of {ref "axioms"}[the cohomology axioms chapter].
Write
$$`B=\bigoplus_{w\in\mathbb{Z}}H^{0,w}(k),\qquad C(X)=\bigoplus_{(p,w)\in\mathbb{Z}^2}H^{p,w}(X)`
for the cohomology of the point and the total cohomology of $`X`, with coefficient ring $`R=B_0`.

The _Orlik–Solomon algebra_ $`\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})` is the quotient of the exterior algebra over $`B` on generators $`e_H`, one for each hyperplane, by a two-sided ideal.
For an ordered list $`I=(H_1,\ldots,H_s)` of distinct hyperplanes, put $`e_I=e_{H_1}\cdots e_{H_s}` and
$$`\partial e_I=\sum_{j=1}^s(-1)^{j-1}e_{H_1}\cdots\widehat{e_{H_j}}\cdots e_{H_s}.`
The ideal is generated by $`e_I` when $`\bigcap I` is empty, and by $`\partial e_I` when $`\bigcap I` is nonempty of codimension less than $`s`.
An equation $`f_H` of a hyperplane is invertible on $`U_{\mathcal{A}}`, so it has a logarithmic class $`[f_H]` of bidegree $`(1,1)`, which {ref "logarithms"}[is constructed from the axioms].

:::statement "Main theorem, affine case"
Let $`H^{\bullet,\bullet}` be a cohomology theory over $`k` that satisfies the axioms.
For every finite-dimensional affine space and every arrangement $`\mathcal{A}` in it:

 * *The total ring.* There is an isomorphism of $`B`-algebras
   $$`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}:\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }C(U_{\mathcal{A}}),\qquad e_H\longmapsto[f_H],`
   which preserves the bigrading, with $`e_H` in bidegree $`(1,1)` and $`B_w` in bidegree $`(0,w)`. The formula holds for every equation $`f_H` of $`H`, so the map does not depend on the equations.
 * *The groups.* For all integers $`p\ge0` and $`w` there is a canonical isomorphism of $`R`-modules
   $$`H^{p,w}(U_{\mathcal{A}})\simeq\operatorname{OS}_R^p(\mathcal{A})\otimes_R B_{w-p},`
   and $`H^{p,w}(U_{\mathcal{A}})=0` for $`p<0` and for $`p>n`.
 * *The diagonal.* There is a canonical isomorphism of graded $`R`-algebras
   $$`\operatorname{OS}_R(\mathcal{A})\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\bigoplus_{q\ge0}H^{q,q}(U_{\mathcal{A}}).`

The ring $`R` is arbitrary: it can be $`\mathbb{Z}`, $`\mathbb{Z}/2` or the zero ring, $`2` is never assumed to be invertible, and $`B` need not be flat over $`R`.
No generator of any Tate line is chosen, and $`U_{\mathcal{A}}` may have no $`k`-points at all.
This is {bp "thm:affine"}[] together with {bp "cor:ordinary"}[].
:::

For the projective case, let $`\mathcal{P}` be a nonempty arrangement of hyperplanes in $`\mathbb{P}^n_k`, with equations $`\ell_i` and central cone $`c\mathcal{P}`.
All the intersections of the cone are nonempty, so the signed boundary $`\partial` descends to $`\operatorname{OS}_B(c\mathcal{P})`.
Its kernel $`\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P})` is the subalgebra generated by the differences $`e_i-e_j`.

:::statement "Main theorem, projective case"
With the same theory, for every nonempty arrangement $`\mathcal{P}` in $`\mathbb{P}^n_k`:

 * *The total ring.* There is an isomorphism of $`B`-algebras
   $$`\overline{\Phi}_{\mathcal{P}}:\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P})\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }C(U_{\mathcal{P}}),\qquad e_i-e_j\longmapsto[\ell_i/\ell_j],`
   which preserves the bigrading. It is defined with no hyperplane at infinity, and it does not depend on the equations.
 * *The diagonal.* There is a canonical isomorphism of graded $`R`-algebras
   $$`\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_R(\mathcal{P})\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\bigoplus_{q\ge0}H^{q,q}(U_{\mathcal{P}}).`

This is {bp "thm:projective"}[], and {ref "projective"}[its chapter] explains the construction.
:::

*The same theorems in Lean.*
The code on this page is elaborated against the library every time the book is built, so it cannot drift from the library.
An `example` asks Lean to check that the term after `:=` has the type stated before it.
The type is the mathematical statement, and the term is the library's declaration.
First come the hypotheses, in which the cohomology theory is the argument `T`.

```lean -show
noncomputable section
open OrlikSolomon OrlikSolomon.SmoothAlg OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra
open scoped TensorProduct DirectSum
universe u v w z
```

```lean
variable
  -- the base field
  {k : Type u} [Field k]
  -- the coefficient ring
  {R : Type v} [CommRing R]
  -- the cohomology of the point, with its weight pieces
  {B : Type w} [CommRing B] [Algebra R B]
  {ℬ : ℤ → Submodule R B} [GradedAlgebra ℬ]
  -- the cohomology theory: this is the hypothesis
  (T : OrdinaryTheory.{u, v, w, z} k ℬ)
  -- a finite-dimensional space and an arrangement in it
  {V : Type u} [AddCommGroup V] [Module k V]
  [FiniteDimensional k V]
  (𝒜 : Arrangement k V)
```

The total ring, then the two facts that make it graded and canonical.

```lean
example : OSAlgebra B 𝒜 ≃ₐ[B] T.C.obj (complement 𝒜) :=
  T.affineEquiv 𝒜

example :
    IsHomogeneousOfShift (bigrading 𝒜 ℬ)
      (T.grading (complement 𝒜)) 0 (T.affineEquiv 𝒜) :=
  T.isHomogeneousOfShift_affineEquiv 𝒜

example {f : V →ᵃ[k] k} (H : 𝒜) (hf : IsEquation f H) :
    T.affineEquiv 𝒜 (generator B 𝒜 H) =
      T.logClass (complementUnit 𝒜 f (hf.covers H.2)) :=
  T.affineEquiv_generator_of_isEquation H hf
```

The groups, and their vanishing outside the range $`0\le p\le n`.

```lean
example (p : ℕ) (w : ℤ) :
    degree R 𝒜 p ⊗[R] ℬ (w - p) ≃ₗ[R]
      T.grading (complement 𝒜) ((p : ℤ), w) :=
  T.ordinaryComponentEquiv 𝒜 p w

example {p w : ℤ}
    (hp : p < 0 ∨ (Module.finrank k V : ℤ) < p) :
    T.grading (complement 𝒜) (p, w) = ⊥ :=
  T.grading_complement_eq_bot hp
```

The diagonal.

```lean
example :
    OSAlgebra R 𝒜 ≃ₐ[R]
      ⨁ q : ℕ, diagonalGrading
        (T.grading (complement 𝒜)) q :=
  T.diagonalEquiv 𝒜
```

For the projective theorem, the arrangement lives in a vector space $`W` of dimension $`n+1`.
The hypothesis `[Nonempty P]` is what excludes the empty arrangement.

```lean
variable
  {W : Type u} [AddCommGroup W] [Module k W]
  [FiniteDimensional k W]
  (P : ProjectiveArrangement k W) [Nonempty P]

example :
    projectiveAlgebra B P.cone_isCentral ≃ₐ[B]
      T.C.obj (projectiveComplement P) :=
  T.projectiveEquiv P

example :
    projectiveAlgebra R P.cone_isCentral ≃ₐ[R]
      ⨁ q : ℕ, diagonalGrading
        (T.grading (projectiveComplement P)) q :=
  T.projectiveDiagonalEquiv P
```

To read these statements, use the following dictionary.

:::table +header
*
 * Lean
 * Mathematics
*
 * {name OrlikSolomon.Arrangement}`Arrangement` `k V`
 * a finite set of hyperplanes in the finite-dimensional $`k`-vector space $`V`, regarded as an affine space
*
 * {name OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory}`OrdinaryTheory` `k ℬ`
 * a cohomology theory on smooth $`k`-algebras with coefficients $`B`, graded by the pieces `ℬ w` $`=B_w`
*
 * {name OrlikSolomon.SmoothAlg.complement}`complement` `𝒜`
 * the object $`U_{\mathcal{A}}`, given by its coordinate ring
*
 * `T.C.obj X`
 * the total cohomology $`C(X)`
*
 * `T.grading X (p, w)`
 * the group $`H^{p,w}(X)`, as an $`R`-submodule of $`C(X)`
*
 * {name OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra}`OSAlgebra` `B 𝒜`
 * the algebra $`\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})`
*
 * {name OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.degree}`degree` `R 𝒜 p`
 * its part $`\operatorname{OS}_R^p(\mathcal{A})` of exterior degree $`p`
*
 * {name OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.bigrading}`bigrading` `𝒜 ℬ`
 * the bigrading of $`\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})`, with pieces indexed by $`(p,w)`
*
 * {name OrlikSolomon.diagonalGrading}`diagonalGrading` `𝒢 q`
 * the piece `𝒢 (q, q)` of a bigrading `𝒢`
*
 * {name OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.logClass}`T.logClass` `u`
 * the class $`[u]` of a unit $`u` of a coordinate ring
*
 * {name OrlikSolomon.IsHomogeneousOfShift}`IsHomogeneousOfShift` `ℳ 𝒩 a f`
 * $`f` sends $`\mathcal{M}_i` into $`\mathcal{N}_{i+a}` for every $`i`
*
 * {name OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement}`ProjectiveArrangement` `k W`
 * a finite set of hyperplanes through the origin of $`W`, regarded as hyperplanes in its projectivization
*
 * {name OrlikSolomon.SmoothAlg.projectiveComplement}`projectiveComplement` `P`
 * the object $`U_{\mathcal{P}}`, given by its coordinate ring
*
 * {name OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveAlgebra}`projectiveAlgebra` `B P.cone_isCentral`
 * the algebra $`\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P})`, the kernel of $`\partial`, where `P.cone_isCentral` is the proof that the cone is central
*
 * `≃ₐ[B]`, `≃ₗ[R]`
 * an isomorphism of $`B`-algebras, of $`R`-modules
*
 * `⊗[R]`, `⨁ q : ℕ`
 * tensor product over $`R`, direct sum over $`q\ge0`
*
 * `(p : ℤ)`, `⊥`
 * the natural number $`p` regarded as an integer, the zero submodule
:::

Each of these isomorphisms is a definition in Lean, since an isomorphism is data, and its type is the statement.
Each takes the theory `T` as an explicit argument.
Beyond that, each depends only on the standard axioms of Lean's logic, as the following output shows, and the build fails if this output changes.
The hypothesis on cohomology is not among these axioms: it is the argument `T`, which is why the theorems are conditional, as explained below.

```lean (name := axiomsOfAffineEquiv)
#print axioms OrdinaryTheory.affineEquiv
```
```leanOutput axiomsOfAffineEquiv
'OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.affineEquiv' depends on axioms: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]
```

```lean (name := axiomsOfComponentEquiv)
#print axioms OrdinaryTheory.ordinaryComponentEquiv
```
```leanOutput axiomsOfComponentEquiv
'OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.ordinaryComponentEquiv' depends on axioms: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]
```

```lean (name := axiomsOfDiagonalEquiv)
#print axioms OrdinaryTheory.diagonalEquiv
```
```leanOutput axiomsOfDiagonalEquiv
'OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.diagonalEquiv' depends on axioms: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]
```

```lean (name := axiomsOfProjectiveEquiv)
#print axioms OrdinaryTheory.projectiveEquiv
```
```leanOutput axiomsOfProjectiveEquiv
'OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.projectiveEquiv' depends on axioms: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]
```

```lean (name := axiomsOfProjectiveDiagonalEquiv)
#print axioms OrdinaryTheory.projectiveDiagonalEquiv
```
```leanOutput axiomsOfProjectiveDiagonalEquiv
'OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.projectiveDiagonalEquiv' depends on axioms: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]
```

*What is conditional.*
Both theorems are conditional, and this is the most important thing to understand about them.
The cohomology theory is the argument `T`, an arbitrary value of the structure {name OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory}`OrdinaryTheory`, whose fields are the axioms: products, a dimension axiom at the point, affine-line invariance, and compatible localization and divisor purity.
The theorems hold for every value of this structure, and they say nothing about any cohomology theory until someone supplies such a value for that theory.

*No nonzero value of this structure has been constructed in Lean.*
The only values in the library are zero theories, with zero coefficients, and these test edge cases without touching the substantive axioms.
That the axioms hold for complex singular cohomology has been checked by hand, field by field, and the checks are recorded in the comments of the source.
For geometric $`\ell`-adic cohomology the source records the analogous interpretation, together with what an instance would still have to supply.
These are mathematical arguments and not formal proofs.
So the library does not yet contain a theorem about singular or $`\ell`-adic cohomology.
To obtain one, you must construct a value of the structure, and {bp "lem:standard-bridge"}[the bridge] reduces this to the familiar degreewise inputs: groups, products, pullbacks, localization with supports and oriented purity.
{ref "axioms"}[The chapter on the axioms] explains each field, the checks and the bridge.
A de Rham instance is in progress, following the [second blueprint](https://github.com/adamtopaz/OrlikSolomon/blob/main/blueprint/de_rham_instance_blueprint.tex), as described at the end of this page.
It is not part of what is proved here.

The axioms are deliberately much weaker than the conclusion.
No axiom asserts that logarithmic classes exist or generate, that the arrangement relations hold, that residues are surjective, that Gysin maps vanish, that a deletion sequence is short exact, that anything is free, or the structure theorems themselves.
All of these are outputs of the proof.

*What is outside the theorems.*
 * Hyperplanes are defined over $`k`. Nonsplit arrangements are not considered.
 * The dimension axiom says that the point has cohomology only in cohomological degree zero. Arithmetic cohomology over a nonclosed field usually fails it, and so do many generalized theories, so they are outside these theorems. The blueprint's separate additive treatment of such theories by a stable splitting is {ref "generalized"}[deferred]: it is out of scope for now and nothing about it is formalized.
 * In the projective case the empty arrangement is excluded. There is no hyperplane to put at infinity, the space is $`\mathbb{P}^n` itself, and its cohomology needs a projective bundle formula that the axioms do not provide.

*How this book is tied to the code.*
Every result appears in a box that shows a status and its label in the [blueprint](https://github.com/adamtopaz/OrlikSolomon/blob/main/blueprint/arrangement_cohomology_lean_blueprint.tex), or, for the de Rham instance, in the [second blueprint](https://github.com/adamtopaz/OrlikSolomon/blob/main/blueprint/de_rham_instance_blueprint.tex):
 * *Planned* means that the result is in scope and no Lean declaration is attached to it yet. The text describes what is intended, and nothing has been checked.
 * *Deferred* means that the result is in the blueprint but outside the current scope of the project.
 * *Partly formalized* means that the box names Lean declarations for part of the result, shows their statements with links to their source, and says what is missing.
 * *Formalized* means that the box names Lean declarations and shows their statements with links to their source, and that review of the change found that they cover the whole result.

The declarations of a box are collapsed under a line at its foot, so that a chapter reads as mathematics.
Open that line to compare them with the text.

*What the build checks.*
A box cannot claim the last two statuses by assertion, because they follow from naming declarations.
For each named declaration, the build of this book checks only that it exists, and that it depends on no `sorry` and on no axiom other than Lean's standard `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`.
A second check fails the build if either blueprint has a node that the book does not present, if the book presents a node twice, or if a label is used twice, even across the two blueprints.
The {ref "status"}[status page] is generated from the same data.
Lean code in the text, such as the statements above, is elaborated when the book is built.
So are the names of declarations that the text sets in Lean's highlighting, and the build fails if one of them does not resolve.

*What the build does not check.*
The build does not check that the named declarations cover the statement in their box.
It would accept any proved declaration there, even one that has nothing to do with the result.
It also does not check any prose, inside or outside the boxes.
Both of these are checked by human or agent review of each change, not by the build, and review can make mistakes.
So do not read *Formalized* as a machine-checked claim about the words in a box.
What the build has checked is the declarations shown there, and you can compare their statements with the text yourself.

*A de Rham instance in progress.*
The status lines at the top of this page count two blueprints separately.
The second plans a value of the structure of axioms: algebraic de Rham cohomology over a field of characteristic zero, which would show that the axioms have a nonzero model and would give a form of the theorem with no hypothetical cohomology theory in it.
Some of its algebraic foundations are formalized, but the library does not yet contain this instance.
{ref "de-rham"}[Its chapter] presents the plan.
The main theorem does not depend on it: the dependence runs the other way.

